  <h2>Do BMI Categories Change for Older Adults?</h2>
  <p>The WHO BMI classification system does not officially change at age 65. The categories — underweight (below 18.5), healthy weight (18.5–24.9), overweight (25.0–29.9), and obese (30.0 and above) — remain the same for all adults aged 20 and older. No major clinical guideline body (WHO, CDC, AHA) has issued an official, universally adopted revision of adult BMI thresholds for seniors.</p>
  <p>However, epidemiological evidence accumulated over two decades has substantially complicated the picture. The relationship between BMI and mortality in adults over 65 is not the same J-shaped curve seen in younger adults. The nadir of mortality risk — the point of lowest all-cause mortality — shifts upward with age. Multiple large cohort studies and meta-analyses, including Winter et al. (<em>American Journal of Clinical Nutrition</em>, 2014), consistently find that adults over 65 with BMI in the <strong>23.0–29.9 range</strong> have the lowest mortality risk. Some data suggest optimal BMI levels up to <strong>31–32 for older females</strong> specifically.</p>
  <p>This phenomenon — where a BMI technically classified as "overweight" or "obese" is associated with better survival outcomes in older populations — is referred to as the <strong>obesity paradox</strong>. The proposed mechanisms include: metabolic reserve during illness (hospitalized older adults with higher BMI tolerate acute illness better), protective effect of muscle mass, reduced osteoporosis and fracture risk, and survivor bias (those with life-shortening obesity may have died before reaching 65).</p>
  <p>Clinically, this means that a 72-year-old with BMI 27 should not be treated as if they have the same risk profile as a 35-year-old with BMI 27. The priority clinical concern for older adults is not moderate adiposity — it is the precipitous loss of skeletal muscle mass and function known as <strong>sarcopenia</strong>.</p>

  <h2>Why BMI Can Be Less Accurate With Age</h2>
  <p>BMI's fundamental limitation — its inability to distinguish fat mass from fat-free mass — becomes more clinically consequential with each decade of aging. At age 70, the same BMI that read as healthy at age 30 represents substantially less muscle mass and more adipose tissue. Longitudinal data from the Baltimore Longitudinal Study of Aging confirm that fat mass increases and lean mass decreases continuously from the third decade onward, with the rate of muscle loss accelerating after age 60.</p>
  <p>Adults lose approximately <strong>3–8% of muscle mass per decade</strong> after age 30. By age 80, a sedentary man may have lost 30–40% of his peak muscle mass; a woman loses less but still faces substantial decline, partly buffered by estrogen's anabolic effects until menopause. This loss is called <strong>sarcopenia</strong> when it reaches clinically significant levels, and it dramatically increases the risk of falls, fractures, disability, and premature death.</p>
  <p>The critical clinical problem is that BMI can remain stable — or even increase — during this process if fat mass increases to compensate for muscle loss. An older adult at "healthy" BMI (18.5–24.9) with severe sarcopenia may have a metabolically worse profile than an older adult at BMI 27 with maintained muscle mass. BMI cannot detect this distinction.</p>

  <h2>Low BMI, Weight Loss, and Malnutrition Risk</h2>
  <p>In older adults, unintentional weight loss is a more immediate clinical concern than high BMI for many patients. The Rotterdam Study (a landmark prospective cohort of 5,888 adults aged 55 and older, following participants for over 20 years) and subsequent studies consistently show that <strong>BMI below 22 in adults over 65</strong> is associated with elevated mortality risk — often reflecting malnutrition, undiagnosed chronic illness, or advanced sarcopenia.</p>
  <p>Unintentional weight loss of more than <strong>5% of body weight in 12 months</strong> in an older adult is a clinical red flag that warrants investigation. Common causes include malignancy, depression, malabsorption, medication side effects, and advanced dementia-related dysphagia. The nutritional target for underweight older adults is typically <strong>1.0–1.2 g of protein per kg of actual body weight</strong> per day, with higher targets (1.2–1.5 g/kg) for those experiencing active sarcopenia or frailty.</p>
  <p>Dietary recommendations for underweight older adults prioritize calorie density and protein quality: full-fat dairy, legumes, nuts, eggs, fish, and calorie-dense whole foods rather than sugary drinks or ultra-processed "empty calories."</p>

  <h2>Muscle Mass, Waist Size, and Function Matter More Than BMI</h2>
  <p>The most clinically meaningful measurements for older adults are:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Waist circumference and waist-to-height ratio:</strong> A waist-to-height ratio above 0.5 signals elevated cardiometabolic risk in older adults, and above 0.6 signals substantially elevated risk — regardless of BMI. The ratio normalizes for height, which is important as older adults frequently lose vertebral height with age-related osteoporosis.</li>
    <li><strong>Grip strength:</strong> A simple proxy for overall muscle function. Low grip strength is one of the three components (along with low muscle mass and low physical performance) used in the EWGSOP2 diagnostic criteria for sarcopenia. Grip strength below 27 kg in men and 16 kg in women warrants further assessment.</li>
    <li><strong>Chair stand test (5 times sit-to-stand):</strong> A functional screen for lower-extremity muscle power. Taking more than 15 seconds to complete five stands is associated with mobility limitation and elevated mortality risk.</li>
    <li><strong>Gait speed:</strong> Gait speed below 0.8 m/s over a 4-meter course is an independent predictor of hospitalization, disability, and mortality in older adults.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The Rotterdam Study data on sarcopenic obesity are particularly striking: participants with probable sarcopenia had a hazard ratio for all-cause mortality of <strong>1.29</strong>; those with confirmed sarcopenia had an HR of <strong>1.93</strong>; and those with sarcopenic obesity (two altered body composition components) faced a mortality HR of <strong>2.84</strong>. The combination of high fat mass and critically low muscle mass in an older adult represents a synergistic mortality risk that BMI alone would miss.</p>

  <h2>How to Interpret Your Result Safely</h2>
  <p>For adults over 65:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>BMI 22–27</strong> is a reasonable target range for most older adults, with the understanding that the upper end of this range is not clinically concerning in the way it would be for a younger adult.</li>
    <li><strong>BMI above 30</strong> (obese) still carries elevated cardiometabolic risk and functional limitation risk (osteoarthritis, reduced mobility, sleep apnea) in older adults — but the mortality curve flattens.</li>
    <li><strong>BMI below 22</strong> warrants investigation for underlying causes, including malnutrition, sarcopenia, malignancy, or chronic disease.</li>
    <li><strong>Sudden changes in weight</strong> (loss or gain of more than 5% in 6–12 months) are more concerning than a static BMI reading and should prompt clinical evaluation.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When to Talk With a Healthcare Professional</h2>
  <p>Engage a geriatrician, primary care physician, or registered dietitian if: BMI is below 22 or above 35, there has been unintentional weight loss, grip strength has declined noticeably, falls have increased in frequency, or there is concern about functional mobility (walking, climbing stairs, rising from a chair). The American Geriatrics Society's 2023 Healthy Aging guidelines recommend resistance training 2–3 times per week as the most evidence-based intervention for preserving independence and muscle mass in older adults.</p>

